        "answer": {
          "short": "$n^2\\equiv0$ or $1\\pmod3$.\n\nWrite $n=3k+r$, where $r=0,1,2$; then $n^2\\equiv r^2\\pmod3$.",
          "worked": "Write $n=3k+r$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2&=(3k+r)^2\\\\n^2&=9k^2+6kr+r^2\\\\n^2&\\equiv r^2\\pmod3.\\end{align*}$$\nThe possible residues are\n$$\\begin{align*}0^2&\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\\\1^2&\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\\\2^2&\\equiv1\\pmod3.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore every integer square is congruent to $0$ or $1$ modulo $3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that the squares of three consecutive integers can never sum to a perfect square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The sum is $3n^2+2\\equiv2\\pmod3$.\n\nBy part (a), a perfect square has residue $0$ or $1$, so this sum cannot be a perfect square.",
          "worked": "Let the three consecutive integers be $n-1$, $n$ and $n+1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(n-1)^2+n^2+(n+1)^2&=n^2-2n+1+n^2+n^2+2n+1\\\\(n-1)^2+n^2+(n+1)^2&=3n^2+2\\\\(n-1)^2+n^2+(n+1)^2&\\equiv2\\pmod3.\\end{align*}$$\nBy part (a), every perfect square has residue $0$ or $1$ modulo $3$. Therefore the sum cannot be a perfect square.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
